# CODEOWNERS.example — copy to .github/CODEOWNERS (or docs/CODEOWNERS) and replace the
# team handles. GitHub then requests review from the owning team automatically and, with
# branch protection's "require review from code owners", makes that review blocking.
#
# Scoping: the LAST matching pattern wins, so order from broad to specific. Scope
# ownership to the design system's directories only — a root "*" rule makes the DS team
# a bottleneck for every PR in the repo, which is how teams learn to route around it.

# The system itself: components, tokens, and the build scripts that generate the registry.
/src/design-system/                 @your-org/design-system
/tokens/                            @your-org/design-system
/scripts/build-registry.mjs         @your-org/design-system

# Governance docs and RFCs — same owners, so proposals land in front of the maintainers.
/governance/                        @your-org/design-system

# Lint configs encode system policy; changing them changes the rules for everyone.
/lint/                              @your-org/design-system

# Generated artifacts: owned so a hand-edit (the classic drift vector) gets caught in
# review. The registry check in CI is the real guard; this is the human backstop.
/.design-system/                    @your-org/design-system

# Example of narrowing: tokens also need a design sign-off, not just engineering.
# Later rules override earlier ones, so this adds the second team for tokens only.
# /tokens/*.tokens.json             @your-org/design-system @your-org/brand-design
